      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Rewrite $\\frac{3}{2}-3z+z^2$ in the form $(z+a)^2+b$. Find the signed numbers $a$ and $b$.\n\n$a=$ {{field-1}}\n\n$b=$ {{field-2}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Put the quadratic term first: $\\frac{3}{2}-3z+z^2=z^2-3z+\\frac{3}{2}$.\n\nThe half of the linear coefficient is $-\\frac{3}{2}$. **Add and subtract** its square so the expression keeps its value.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz^2-3z+\\frac{3}{2}&=\\underbrace{z^2-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}z+\\frac{9}{4}}_{\\text{perfect square}}-\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\\n&=\\left(z-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)^2-\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\\n&=\\left(z-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)^2-\\frac{3}{4}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minus sign inside the square produces the required linear term $-3z$.\n\nComparing with $(z+a)^2+b$ gives $a=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and $b=-\\frac{3}{4}$.",
